// INT8 Symmetric Quantization Kernel
//
// STATUS: blocked. Neither kernel typechecks today. The blocker is a single
// missing language feature: numeric conversion between element types.
//
// Probing the typechecker gives a precise picture:
//
//   Tensor[f32] -> Tensor[f32]   OK
//   Tensor[i8]  -> Tensor[i8]    OK
//   Tensor[i8]  -> Tensor[f32]   "type mismatch: expected `Float`, found `Int`"
//   Tensor[f32] -> Tensor[i8]    "type mismatch: expected `Int`, found `Float`"
//
// `as i8` is not an escape hatch: there is no `as` token in the lexer, so it
// is a parse error ("expected '}', found as").
//
// So symmetric INT8 quantization cannot be written in naso today, because
// quantizing is precisely an f32 -> i8 narrowing. The bodies below are the
// form each kernel will take once conversions exist; they are kept legible so
// the intent is not lost, but they do not compile yet and must not be
// presented as working.
//
// Three further gaps, found while checking these bodies:
//
// 1. No `proof` block. `proof` is not a keyword anywhere in the lexer, so
//    `proof { .. }` fails with "expected '}', found '{'". The verification
//    condition below is a comment and is NOT checked by the prover.
//
// 2. Symbolic extents. `Tensor[T, N]` requires a literal; `N` as a
//    parameter is a parse error. The loop domain is therefore fixed at
//    compile time rather than derived from an argument.
//
// 3. scale was originally `[0]`, which is right in spirit (read-only across
//    the loop) but wrong in practice: a `[0]` variable is erased, and the
//    typechecker correctly rejects it in a runtime position with "erased
//    variable `scale` used at runtime". Read-only-across-the-loop is not yet
//    expressible; `mut` does not mean it either.

/// Quantize f32 -> i8 with a symmetric scale. BLOCKED: needs a cast.
///
/// The cast can overflow unless the precondition holds, which is what the
/// proof below would establish:
///
///   forall i in 0..1024 { abs(input[i]) <= 127.0 * scale }
///
/// so the clamp is load-bearing, not defensive decoration.
fn quantize_int8_symmetric(
    input: [1] Tensor[f32, 1024],
    output: inout [1] Tensor[i8, 1024],
    scale: f32
) {
    // proof {
    //     assert(forall (i in 0..1024) { abs(input[i]) <= 127.0 * scale });
    // }

    forall i in 0..1024 {
        let v = round(input[i] / scale);
        output[i] = clamp(v, -128.0, 127.0) as i8;
    }
}

/// Dequantize i8 -> f32. BLOCKED: needs the same cast, in reverse.
fn dequantize_int8_symmetric(
    input: [1] Tensor[i8, 1024],
    output: inout [1] Tensor[f32, 1024],
    scale: f32
) {
    forall i in 0..1024 {
        let v = input[i] as f32;
        output[i] = v * scale;
    }
}

/// Round-trip scaffolding that does typecheck today: f32 -> f32, using the
/// same prelude intrinsics a quantizer needs. Useful as a smoke test that
/// forall loops, tensor indexing and the math builtins all lower.
fn normalize_f32(
    input: [1] Tensor[f32, 1024],
    output: inout [1] Tensor[f32, 1024],
    scale: f32
) {
    forall i in 0..1024 {
        output[i] = round(input[i] / scale);
    }
}
